{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"oasis-diagnostic","update":{"sessionUpdate":"tool_call_update","toolCallId":"exec:0#5aaf4b2e87934b979112d3fe9fd87c9a","status":"in_progress","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"     0 app/__init__.py\n   132 app/__main__.py\n   230 app/api.py\n  1157 app/builtins.py\n   198 app/decision.py\n   306 app/dsl.py\n   180 app/engines.py\n    81 app/governance.py\n   120 app/host.py\n   241 app/ledger.py\n   145 app/model.py\n   131 app/needs.py\n   133 app/personas.py\n    46 app/seed.py\n    44 app/steward.py\n   243 app/tools.py\n   280 app/ui/app.js\n    95 app/ui/index.html\n    51 app/ui/styles.css\n   319 scripts/demo.py\n    94 scripts/probe.py\n   116 FEATURES.md\n    53 LAUNCH.md\n     4 launch.cmd\n   364 ../devlog.md\n    74 ../conventions-001.md\n  4837 total\n===\n# CONVENTIONS.md — Avatars conventions\n\nAdopted 2026-11-19. Developed in the harness workspace (`C:\\builds\\project\\conventions-001.md`,\n`conventions-sweep-001.md` — the classification rationale lives there).\n\n## The layer\n\nBetween the **prompt** (specified behavior — platform-agnostic, binding by being the\nprompt) and **implementation details** (free choices) sits a third specificity tier:\n**conventions**. (\"Requirement\" is not a defined corpus term — the prompt needs no\ncategory name for what it specifies.)\n\nA convention is a commitment at intermediate specificity. **It can be challenged, but it\ncannot be ignored.** Departures are permitted only as recorded challenges with stated\nreasons; silent non-conformance is a defect — a finding, not a choice. This is the\nenforceable form of M-Convention: conventions are the Schelling points that let\nindependent implementations converge when the prompt doesn't determine an answer.\n\n## Force and mechanics\n\n- **Default-binding.** An implementation follows a convention unless it records a\n  challenge. \"Didn't look\" is non-conformance, not a choice.\n\u001b[0m[... 34 lines omitted ...]\n  defaults and ranked targets.\n- **Not conventions:** prompt-specified behavior and free implementation choices.\n  Owner rulings land wherever the owner places them.\n\n## The convention set (43)\n\n### Records & ledger\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-JsonlLedger | Append-only durable JSONL assertion journal as the ledger realization (converged in two builds) |\n| C-LocalIndices | Stable local indices for persistent objects (avatarID/toolID); labels/encoding free |\n| C-DyadicConfidence | Shared normalization of confidence semantics and threshold discipline; math details challengeabl\u001b[0me\u001b[0m\n |\n| C-CertaintyPrivilege | Certainty-top reserved for user-set values; recency breaks ties (mechanics beneath C-UserSetSu\u001b[0mp\u001b[0m\nremacy) |\n| C-UnmetNeedRecords | Unmet-need floor records (converged in both builds) |\n| C-AcceptanceChecks | Executable adequacy checks + candidate retention + confidence-lowering; encoding free |\n| C-DeterministicMatchReview | Deterministic match *plus* LLM review pairing (the regex itself is free) |\n\n### Sources & retrieval\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-MultiSourceAccess | Capability to access multiple configured sources so distinct personalities carry different info\u001b[0mr\u001b[0m\nmation |\n| C-CompetingSources | Wikipedia and Google as separate competing sources |\n| C-SpecializedListRetrieval | Specialized over general search for list completion — the machinery behind defensible se\u001b[0mt\u001b[0m\n answers |\n| C-ConfiguredAccounts | Gmail, Google Calendar, profiles as configured accounts/sources |\n| C-ContactTags | Specific ledger tags for contacts |\n| C-SourceCeilings | Per-source confidence ceilings; mixed results → derived newer entries (records are immutable) |\n| C-SourceDomains | Domain-described sources; vocabulary/matching free |\n| C-ConnectorSet | The conventional source catalog (17 connectors); narrower is a recorded challenge |\n\n### Tools & work\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-ConvergentOntology | Invokable components as tool-as-data DSL / object.verb commands / closed primitive allow-list \u001b[0m(\u001b[0m\nindependently converged) |\n| C-SetBuild | Geographic set fulfillment — the machinery under S-list-project |\n| C-AgenticProtocol | Tools-invoking-tools protocol |\n| C-StewardAsTool | Stewardship as a configurable, assignable tool |\n| C-UserFacingPriority | Background work yields to user-facing work (policy direction; scheduler free) |\n| C-TemplateDerivedUI | Form/execution-check derivation from templates |\n| C-ExplicitPolicies | Explicit thresholds/budgets/retry limits; each value separately reviewable |\n\n### Surfaces & presentation\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-HostAuthority | Authoritative host + satellite surfaces; transport/topology free unless an exchange agreement pins \u001b[0mi\u001b[0m\nt |\n| C-DesktopShell | Capable of running as its own application with a separate GUI — capability, not the only allowed met\u001b[0mh\u001b[0m\nod; browser display is legitimate, expected-best on satellites |\n| C-FullScreen | Ability to display full-screen — held for portability's sake |\n| C-InspectionSurfaces | Library/Studio/Storefronts-style organization; labels/layout free |\n| C-PresentationGlass | Asset pipeline + portrait frames + ambient motion |\n| C-ConversationArchive | Per-turn archive + topic segments |\n| C-HonestyBanner | Runtime surfaces declare capability honestly (ledger durability, engine/stub status) |\n| C-DeclaredStubs | Failed/stubbed capability is declared honestly — no ok:true-while-failing, no silent degradation |\n| C-LauncherTests | Bench/owner tests via launcher shortcuts with env baked; owner-facing test steps use operator surfa\u001b[0mc\u001b[0m\nes only |\n\n### Voice & personality\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-PerceivableRespondents | Respondent contributions remain individually perceivable; overlapping speech that renders \u001b[0mt\u001b[0m\nhem indistinct requires a recorded challenge |\n| C-SequentialRespondents | Multiple respondents present in sequence, each able to react to earlier contributions |\n| C-SpeechFloor | Serialized playback admission through a host queue — the manner beneath the perceivability floor |\n| C-EngineMapping | Portable preferences vs device mapping separation (AVQR voice guide); engine selection free |\n| C-PersonalityArt | Where generation capability exists, each personality's generated art is unique to that personality\u001b[0m \u001b[0m\n|\n| C-VoiceDiversity | Personalities' voices are diverse and modifiable |\n\n### Governance & exchange\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-UserSetSupremacy | User-set values outrank derived/inferred/source values regardless of support; nothing silently s\u001b[0mu\u001b[0m\npersedes them (platform context may obviate need or method) |\n| C-EthicsGate | Liveness research, objection/compliance flow, gate cards — machinery beneath the prompt-specified imit\u001b[0ma\u001b[0m\ntion mark |\n| C-ExchangeLayers | Multiple encoding layers and channels (2D-barcode encode *and* read is itself a capability step; A\u001b[0mV\u001b[0m\nQR, codebooks, envelopes, postcard batch are separate layers); adopted schemas pin provisionally |\n| C-SupportedOS-\\* | Ranked fleet-target list (each its own convention; ranking itself conventional): **1. Windows 11 ·\u001b[0m \u001b[0m\n2. Android satellite devices · 3. Bluetooth support for Echo devices · 4. Windows 10 · 5. TAB-YOGA satellite · 6. Linux\u001b[0m \u001b[0m\n· 7. Raspberry Pi** |\n\n### Evaluation & test\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-ScenarioEnactment | Named usage scenarios are enacted on real inputs; canned/seeded substitutes don't satisfy witho\u001b[0mu\u001b[0m\nt a recorded challenge |\n| C-CanonicalScenarios | The line's discriminating tests are enacted per version: S-list-project (PA cities), S-persona\u001b[0ml\u001b[0m\nity-create, and the named suite |"}}],"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec","cognition.ai/terminalPreview":true}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"oasis-diagnostic","update":{"sessionUpdate":"tool_call_update","toolCallId":"exec:0#5aaf4b2e87934b979112d3fe9fd87c9a","status":"in_progress","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"     0 app/__init__.py\n   132 app/__main__.py\n   230 app/api.py\n  1157 app/builtins.py\n   198 app/decision.py\n   306 app/dsl.py\n   180 app/engines.py\n    81 app/governance.py\n   120 app/host.py\n   241 app/ledger.py\n   145 app/model.py\n   131 app/needs.py\n   133 app/personas.py\n    46 app/seed.py\n    44 app/steward.py\n   243 app/tools.py\n   280 app/ui/app.js\n    95 app/ui/index.html\n    51 app/ui/styles.css\n   319 scripts/demo.py\n    94 scripts/probe.py\n   116 FEATURES.md\n    53 LAUNCH.md\n     4 launch.cmd\n   364 ../devlog.md\n    74 ../conventions-001.md\n  4837 total\n===\n# CONVENTIONS.md — Avatars conventions\n\nAdopted 2026-11-19. Developed in the harness workspace (`C:\\builds\\project\\conventions-001.md`,\n`conventions-sweep-001.md` — the classification rationale lives there).\n\n## The layer\n\nBetween the **prompt** (specified behavior — platform-agnostic, binding by being the\nprompt) and **implementation details** (free choices) sits a third specificity tier:\n**conventions**. (\"Requirement\" is not a defined corpus term — the prompt needs no\ncategory name for what it specifies.)\n\nA convention is a commitment at intermediate specificity. **It can be challenged, but it\ncannot be ignored.** Departures are permitted only as recorded challenges with stated\nreasons; silent non-conformance is a defect — a finding, not a choice. This is the\nenforceable form of M-Convention: conventions are the Schelling points that let\nindependent implementations converge when the prompt doesn't determine an answer.\n\n## Force and mechanics\n\n- **Default-binding.** An implementation follows a convention unless it records a\n  challenge. \"Didn't look\" is non-conformance, not a choice.\n- **Challengeable.** A challenge states: the convention, the departure, and the reason.\n  Recorded challenges are build evidence and surface at review — the owner's\n  adjudication promotes (into the prompt), retains, or retires (back to implementation\n  choice) the convention.\n- **Not ignorable.** An unrecorded departure counts against the build as a conformance\n  gap — the criterion it costs depends on the convention (parity for realization\n  conventions, completeness for behavior-shaping ones).\n\n## Owner rulings (2026-11-19)\n\n1. **Identifier scheme: C-\\*** — matches the corpus pattern (E-\\*, M-\\*, D-\\*).\n2. **Free departure with recorded challenge.** The harness (and its builds) may ignore a\n   convention provided the departure is **acknowledged, evaluated, and explained** — the\n   record is the duty; review adjudicates afterward. Silent departure remains a defect.\n3. **Conformity is NOT assessed.** It is not a criterion — we do not care which direction\n   a build goes on a convention, only that the choice was acknowledged, evaluated, and\n   explained.\n4. **Test criteria can be conventions.** Evaluation criteria and named test scenarios may\n   themselves be conventional — a build may challenge a criterion or test but must\n   acknowledge, evaluate, and explain the departure.\n5. **Platform context can obviate.** A platform-specific implementation may obviate a\n   convention's *need* or its *method of enactment* (e.g., the platform enforces user-set\n   supremacy or unifies sources elsewhere). That is a legitimate recorded challenge — the\n   convention stands as the default; the platform rationale is the explanation.\n6. **Adopted schemas pin provisionally.** Within their layer (e.g., exchange encodings),\n   an adopted schema pins while adopted — evolving over time, revocable through the\n   schema's own process; departure is always available by not claiming the schema. What\n   is not available: claiming conformance to an agreed contract while breaking it.\n\n## What belongs here (and what doesn't)\n\n- **Conventions:** named usage scenarios and how they're enacted; proven realizations\n  that independent implementations converged on; inherited test/operator conventions;\n  realizations the corpus keeps as reference (reclassified D-\\* records); owner-declared\n  defaults and ranked targets.\n- **Not conventions:** prompt-specified behavior and free implementation choices.\n  Owner rulings land wherever the owner places them.\n\n## The convention set (43)\n\n### Records & ledger\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-JsonlLedger | Append-only durable JSONL assertion journal as the ledger realization (converged in two builds) |\n| C-LocalIndices | Stable local indices for persistent objects (avatarID/toolID); labels/encoding free |\n| C-DyadicConfidence | Shared normalization of confidence semantics and threshold discipline; math details challengeable |\n| C-CertaintyPrivilege | Certainty-top reserved for user-set values; recency breaks ties (mechanics beneath C-UserSetSupremacy) |\n| C-UnmetNeedRecords | Unmet-need floor records (converged in both builds) |\n| C-AcceptanceChecks | Executable adequacy checks + candidate retention + confidence-lowering; encoding free |\n| C-DeterministicMatchReview | Deterministic match *plus* LLM review pairing (the regex itself is free) |\n\n### Sources & retrieval\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-MultiSourceAccess | Capability to access multiple configured sources so distinct personalities carry different information |\n| C-CompetingSources | Wikipedia and Google as separate competing sources |\n| C-SpecializedListRetrieval | Specialized over general search for list completion — the machinery behind defensible set answers |\n| C-ConfiguredAccounts | Gmail, Google Calendar, profiles as configured accounts/sources |\n| C-ContactTags | Specific ledger tags for contacts |\n| C-SourceCeilings | Per-source confidence ceilings; mixed results → derived newer entries (records are immutable) |\n| C-SourceDomains | Domain-described sources; vocabulary/matching free |\n| C-ConnectorSet | The conventional source catalog (17 connectors); narrower is a recorded challenge |\n\n### Tools & work\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-ConvergentOntology | Invokable components as tool-as-data DSL / object.verb commands / closed primitive allow-list (independently converged) |\n| C-SetBuild | Geographic set fulfillment — the machinery under S-list-project |\n| C-AgenticProtocol | Tools-invoking-tools protocol |\n| C-StewardAsTool | Stewardship as a configurable, assignable tool |\n| C-UserFacingPriority | Background work yields to user-facing work (policy direction; scheduler free) |\n| C-TemplateDerivedUI | Form/execution-check derivation from templates |\n| C-ExplicitPolicies | Explicit thresholds/budgets/retry limits; each value separately reviewable |\n\n### Surfaces & presentation\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-HostAuthority | Authoritative host + satellite surfaces; transport/topology free unless an exchange agreement pins it |\n| C-DesktopShell | Capable of running as its own application with a separate GUI — capability, not the only allowed method; browser display is legitimate, expected-best on satellites |\n| C-FullScreen | Ability to display full-screen — held for portability's sake |\n| C-InspectionSurfaces | Library/Studio/Storefronts-style organization; labels/layout free |\n| C-PresentationGlass | Asset pipeline + portrait frames + ambient motion |\n| C-ConversationArchive | Per-turn archive + topic segments |\n| C-HonestyBanner | Runtime surfaces declare capability honestly (ledger durability, engine/stub status) |\n| C-DeclaredStubs | Failed/stubbed capability is declared honestly — no ok:true-while-failing, no silent degradation |\n| C-LauncherTests | Bench/owner tests via launcher shortcuts with env baked; owner-facing test steps use operator surfaces only |\n\n### Voice & personality\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-PerceivableRespondents | Respondent contributions remain individually perceivable; overlapping speech that renders them indistinct requires a recorded challenge |\n| C-SequentialRespondents | Multiple respondents present in sequence, each able to react to earlier contributions |\n| C-SpeechFloor | Serialized playback admission through a host queue — the manner beneath the perceivability floor |\n| C-EngineMapping | Portable preferences vs device mapping separation (AVQR voice guide); engine selection free |\n| C-PersonalityArt | Where generation capability exists, each personality's generated art is unique to that personality |\n| C-VoiceDiversity | Personalities' voices are diverse and modifiable |\n\n### Governance & exchange\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-UserSetSupremacy | User-set values outrank derived/inferred/source values regardless of support; nothing silently supersedes them (platform context may obviate need or method) |\n| C-EthicsGate | Liveness research, objection/compliance flow, gate cards — machinery beneath the prompt-specified imitation mark |\n| C-ExchangeLayers | Multiple encoding layers and channels (2D-barcode encode *and* read is itself a capability step; AVQR, codebooks, envelopes, postcard batch are separate layers); adopted schemas pin provisionally |\n| C-SupportedOS-\\* | Ranked fleet-target list (each its own convention; ranking itself conventional): **1. Windows 11 · 2. Android satellite devices · 3. Bluetooth support for Echo devices · 4. Windows 10 · 5. TAB-YOGA satellite · 6. Linux · 7. Raspberry Pi** |\n\n### Evaluation & test\n\n| ID | Convention |\n|----|-----------|\n| C-ScenarioEnactment | Named usage scenarios are enacted on real inputs; canned/seeded substitutes don't satisfy without a recorded challenge |\n| C-CanonicalScenarios | The line's discriminating tests are enacted per version: S-list-project (PA cities), S-personality-create, and the named suite |"}}],"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec","terminal_exit":{"terminal_id":"54d749","exit_code":0,"signal":null}}}}}
