{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"oasis-diagnostic","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm extending the PortraitEngine to generate richer, per-persona art. This involves using persona attributes like ID, traits, and interests to create unique compositions, palettes, and frame styles. I'll also implement \"ambient motion\" using SVG animate elements and CSS classes for dynamic portraits. Each persona's art must be demonstrably unique, derived from its specific attributes. I'm also refining temporal reasoning in `app/temporal.py` to better link calendar events with projects, automatically deriving `calendar:linked` assertions and `project:temporal-note` artifacts. Persona responses will be contextually enriched with upcoming events relevant to their interests, and the calendar scan will proactively offer relevant events through personas. Project creation will also support temporal awareness, accepting `dueAt` or `dueInDays` and linking relevant calendar events as evidence."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-21T05:51:24.469539400+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"oasis-diagnostic","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm refining the tool competition mechanism. It now runs all eligible tools for a given need, normalizes their results into a consistent format, and records assertions about the comparison and individual tool outcomes. Ranking is based on decision model features and feedback. I've identified competing pairs like `speech.render` (SAPI vs. pure-Python WAV) and `search.query` (Wikipedia vs. ledger lookup), both producing comparable artifacts and outcomes. The `fulfill_need` process will leverage this competition, selecting the best-ranked tool after each run, creating a dynamic ranking system."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-21T05:51:26.587938800+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"oasis-diagnostic","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm defining three real connectors: `connector.wikipedia` for fetching Wikipedia data, `connector.calendar-ics` to read .ics files as calendar events, and `connector.files` to ingest local directories and text files. These connectors will provide source-evidence assertions with provenance and include a `connector:read` cycle, handling real-world failures like network unreachability."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-21T05:51:29.544866200+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"oasis-diagnostic","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm considering adding an RSS connector as a fourth, bonus connector, since it's only about 30 lines of code. The prompt specifies three required connectors, but more are acceptable. My current plan is Wikipedia (network), ICS (local file), and files (local), with RSS as a potential fourth. I'm also clarifying the definition of \"connector\" within GreyAvatar as a named external source and how these sources feed into personas via `persona.sources` and `C-MultiSourceAccess`."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-21T05:51:31.890358800+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"oasis-diagnostic","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm comparing two tools for fetching city data: `set-members-wikipedia` which parses HTML tables, and `set-members-wikidata` which uses SPARQL queries. Both seem to produce comparable lists of incorporated cities, excluding CDPs as intended by the refined need. The results are stored in `runtime/artifacts/pa-cities.json` as a set of member assertions."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-21T05:51:35.227475400+00:00"}}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"oasis-diagnostic","update":{"sessionUpdate":"agent_thought_chunk","content":{"type":"text","text":"\n\nMy SPARQL query for Pennsylvania cities is flawed. I initially assumed `wd:Q7930989` (city of the United States) was correct, but it seems PA cities are instances of `wd:Q515` (city) or `wd:Q620349` (city of Pennsylvania). A simpler approach is to use the Wikipedia list page, which has a sortable table of all PA cities. I'll examine its structure now."},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/timestamp":"2026-11-21T05:51:35.847380800+00:00"}}}}
